{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_kvgyewthjjrs7cl3baslhsas5cmhcy42b7u2fdlygxyogsgrvyha","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Summary:\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md`: `nm -D` shows 0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols | ✅ diff **empty**, 16/16 both sides |\n| Phase B: every `CONFIGS.md` row passes across randomized inputs | ✅ **65/65** rows |\n| Phase C: every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing differential test | ✅ **54/54** rows |\n| Holds under every feature combination | ✅ 3 combos × 2 profiles, plus 7 C build variants × 2 profiles — **20 full-suite runs, 123 tests each, all green** (`bash verify.sh` → `ALL CHECKS PASSED`) |\n\n## What the library is\n\n`stb_ds.h`'s implementation + two test helpers, one TU, 16 exported symbols. The public header declares only `intput`, but the `.so` exports the whole low-level `stbds_*` API — so the harness **re-implements the `stb_ds` macros** (`hmput`/`shputs`/`hmgeti_ts`/`shdel`/`sh_new_arena`/`arrput`…) on top of the exported functions, including the hash-side↔raw-side pointer bias and the `t[-1]` default element. Every operation deep-compares both libraries: header fields, all element bytes, the entire `stbds_hash_index`, **and every bucket's raw `hash[]`/`index[]`** (comparable because both libs' seed chains advance in lock-step).\n\n## Divergence found and fixed (in the Rust, never the C)\n\n**Profile-dependent fault mode on NULL pointers.** `lib.c` never null-checks a pointer parameter, so `hash_string(NULL,·)`, `hash_bytes(NULL,8,·)`, `stralloc(NULL,·)`, `stralloc(·,NULL)`, `strreset(NULL)`, `hmget_key_ts(…,temp=NULL,…)`, a NULL `key`, and a failing `realloc` inside `arrgrowf` all die with **SIGSEGV**. The Rust used plain `*p`, which trips rustc's null-deref UB check and dies with **SIGABRT** whenever debug assertions are on — the release `.so` matched C, the debug `.so` did not. Those FFI-boundary accesses now use `read_volatile`/`write_volatile`. Verified `signal == SIGSEGV(11)` for both libs in both profiles (8 new rows E3, E47–E54).\n\n## C quirks reproduced, not \"fixed\"\n\n- `arrgrowf(NULL, es, 0, 0)` returns **NULL** — the `min_cap <= arrcap` early return beats the floor-to-4 logic (my initial test assumed otherwise; the C is right).\n- `intput(9)` / `intput(11)` **abort** — the third assert is false. Both libs emit a byte-identical glibc diagnostic (same `__FILE__` path, line, function, expression) and SIGABRT.\n- `mode == 2` makes `hmdel_key` hash the *address* of the key field as a C string → `assert(slot >= 0)` at lib.c:846 fires identically in both.\n- `make_hash_index` never initialises `temp_key` — genuine uninitialised heap data, so it's only compared where the C provably wrote it. Only the *forward* half of the probe loop updates it, so `shputs` can store a stale key pointer; reproduced exactly.\n- Arena `block` saturates at **22**, not 23; `assert(len <= a->remaining)` is *provably unreachable* (proof in `ERRORS.md` E31); `shmode_func` truncates its `int` to `unsigned char`.\n\nArtifacts in `translation/`: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `VERIFICATION.md`, `verify.sh`, `list_features.py`, and 9 test binaries (~4.7k lines). `c_src/` is unmodified (md5s recorded)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":880,"cache_read_input_tokens":328607,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":880,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05384482-1f61-4a19-aaf0-d0cab88dd547","uuid":"3be910dc-cafc-4af3-b9b7-023c0c4b1d76","timestamp":"2026-08-27T11:32:07.782Z"}
